  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nModerating temperatures are expected on Saturday as the surface \nridge pushes off to the east toward the eastern seaboard. Highs are \nforecast to climb back into the upper 80s to near the 90 degree \nmark, or slightly warmer than normal for the date.\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in very good agreement with the synoptic \npattern across the CONUS late this weekend into next week. Anomalous \nmid/upper level ridging will build into the area on Sunday, with 500-\nhPa heights above the 95th percentile. This west-east oriented \nmidlevel ridge very likely will continue through next Thursday, and \npotentially beyond. Clusters of the 500-hPa height pattern show 3 of \nthe 4 clusters (~89% of LREF membership) with this pattern through \nThursday, so there is pretty high confidence that once the \nsummertime heat sets back in Sunday, it will have legs. Mean \ntemperatures at 850 hPa climb well above +20C, and are expected to \nexceed the 95th percentile of climatology. Dewpoint (relative \nhumidity) levels are likely to start off much lower than earlier \nthis summer. While not a \"dry\" heat by any means, it does look like \ndewpoints will stay predominantly in the 60s. Probabilities on the \nLREF never climb above 40% at any time for 70+ dewpoints, though do \nget close in northeast Missouri and west-central Illinois. This is \nwhere there is a bit more uncertainty with temperatures as there \nremains a signal for a frontal boundary to stall, likely just to the \nnorth. This boundary may serve as the focus for showers and \nthunderstorms on the northern periphery of the mid/upper level \nridge. However, for most of the area, there is very high confidence \nin well-above normal temperatures the entire period. High \ntemperatures well into the 90s are forecast each day, and it is \nconceivable that there could be a day (or two) where mixing is deep \nenough with favorable downslope flow off of the Ozark Plateau to \nyield highs nearing the century mark in central/east central \nMissouri, including metropolitan St. Louis. Heat index values should \ntend to climb into midweek, with peak values near or slightly above \n100 degrees expected in some locations.",
